WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.631
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Data Platform and SQL Server

2
00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:07.162
Identity learning path: Entra
logins and the parallel run.

3
00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:09.025
This is the migration itself.

4
00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:11.758
You add the Entra login
beside the Windows one,

5
00:00:11.758 --> 00:00:14.562
prove it works, and only
then turn the old one off.

6
00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:16.650
The module follows runbook R five,

7
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.200
and Chapter 4 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

8
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.240
Take it after Module 3.

9
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.602
Why does the order matter?

10
00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:30.107
On a Category 1 instance,

11
00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:33.991
every Windows and sequel login
gets an Entra login beside it.

12
00:00:33.991 --> 00:00:36.500
The new login is proven
with a real sign-in,

13
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.003
and only then is the old one disabled,
observed for thirty days and dropped.

14
00:00:41.003 --> 00:00:43.290
Two mistakes here can't be taken back.

15
00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:47.803
A wrong mapping locks people out, and
a dropped login can't be rolled back.

16
00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:51.882
So the plan is written as reviewable
T-SQL that executes nothing,

17
00:00:51.882 --> 00:00:54.941
and readiness is judged
from evidence, not silence.

18
00:00:54.941 --> 00:00:57.868
In this module you write a
fictional instance's plan

19
00:00:57.868 --> 00:01:01.030
and judge its parallel run
on day one and day thirty.

20
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.580
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

21
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.892
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

22
00:01:09.892 --> 00:01:13.089
You'll name the three Entra
principal types a login from an

23
00:01:13.089 --> 00:01:16.404
external provider maps to, and
the login type each one shows.

24
00:01:16.404 --> 00:01:20.647
You'll turn a reviewed mapping into
create, cutover and rollback scripts,

25
00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:25.243
and say why a Windows group is denied
connect sequel instead of being disabled.

26
00:01:25.243 --> 00:01:28.810
You'll state the parallel run's
order and its exit criteria,

27
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.892
and why local sessions don't count.

28
00:01:30.892 --> 00:01:33.422
And you'll judge a parallel
run from snapshots,

29
00:01:33.422 --> 00:01:35.418
and say what comes after the disable.

30
00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:37.893
Before you start, finish Module 3.

31
00:01:37.893 --> 00:01:43.260
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on the kit's
fixtures, with no instance and no tenant.

32
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.310
Unit 2 starts with the principals.

33
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.348
Create login, from external provider, maps
a login to one of three Entra principals.

34
00:01:54.348 --> 00:01:56.823
A user, named by its U P N.

35
00:01:56.823 --> 00:01:59.785
A security group, named
by its display name.

36
00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:02.585
Or an application, also by display name.

37
00:02:02.585 --> 00:02:04.497
In sys dot server principals,

38
00:02:04.497 --> 00:02:08.242
users and applications show as
type E, and groups as type X.

39
00:02:08.242 --> 00:02:12.385
Group logins are the target,
because they remove per-user churn.

40
00:02:12.385 --> 00:02:16.062
And an application's sequel login
with a stored password becomes

41
00:02:16.062 --> 00:02:19.398
an Entra application login,
preferably a managed identity.

42
00:02:19.398 --> 00:02:22.898
Create user, from login, then
makes the database user.

43
00:02:22.898 --> 00:02:26.798
One more thing to keep in mind:
the engine has no M F A logic.

44
00:02:26.798 --> 00:02:30.460
Conditional Access decides
before the token is issued.

45
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.742
The kit's login plan reads one
instance from the audit snapshot,

46
00:02:37.742 --> 00:02:39.148
and the reviewed mapping.

47
00:02:39.148 --> 00:02:42.511
It leaves out the S A account
and the built-in principals.

48
00:02:42.511 --> 00:02:46.648
A login with no mapping is written
as unresolved, instead of a guess.

49
00:02:46.648 --> 00:02:49.182
It mirrors server and database roles,

50
00:02:49.182 --> 00:02:52.861
and writes three scripts:
create, cutover and rollback.

51
00:02:52.861 --> 00:02:54.348
It executes nothing.

52
00:02:54.348 --> 00:02:58.493
It also warns when a principal would
hold more than one database role,

53
00:02:58.493 --> 00:03:02.129
or D B owner or sys-admin, because
the data platform book gives

54
00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:05.910
each group exactly one role, on
one database, in one environment.

55
00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.985
A warning doesn't stop the plan.

56
00:03:07.985 --> 00:03:12.085
It's for the identity engineer to
split the group, or record why not.

57
00:03:12.085 --> 00:03:15.023
What the plan doesn't mirror
is explicit permissions,

58
00:03:15.023 --> 00:03:17.160
default schemas and object ownership.

59
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.860
Runbook R five's queries read those,
and the D B A adds them by hand.

60
00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.948
Unit 3 is the parallel run.

61
00:03:27.948 --> 00:03:32.292
The order is create, validate,
disable, observe for thirty days,

62
00:03:32.292 --> 00:03:35.309
then drop, and it's
required for production.

63
00:03:35.309 --> 00:03:38.922
While both logins work, there
are three exit criteria.

64
00:03:38.922 --> 00:03:42.561
Every application and job also
appears on its Entra login.

65
00:03:42.561 --> 00:03:44.948
No remote N T L M remains.

66
00:03:44.948 --> 00:03:47.872
And nothing still arrives
only on the legacy login.

67
00:03:47.872 --> 00:03:52.997
Connections made on the server itself
always report N T L M, so they don't count.

68
00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:55.098
A snapshot is only a moment.

69
00:03:55.098 --> 00:03:59.036
Sequel Server Audit's successful
login group records the whole window.

70
00:03:59.036 --> 00:04:04.097
The cutover disables a Windows user or a
sequel login, and the rollback enables it.

71
00:04:04.097 --> 00:04:06.562
A Windows group can't
be disabled that way,

72
00:04:06.562 --> 00:04:10.548
so the kit denies it connect sequel,
and the rollback grants it back.

73
00:04:10.548 --> 00:04:11.884
Nothing is dropped.

74
00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:15.685
The S A account is disabled
and renamed, never migrated.

75
00:04:15.685 --> 00:04:19.060
And the kit's judge reports
readiness for each legacy login.

76
00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.160
It never disables anything.

77
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:25.847
Try it.

78
00:04:25.847 --> 00:04:30.798
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7, from the kit's
folder, on the kit's fictional estate.

79
00:04:30.798 --> 00:04:35.531
First, make this run's results folder:
its name is the time you start,

80
00:04:35.531 --> 00:04:38.423
in U T C, so every run
keeps its own results.

81
00:04:38.423 --> 00:04:41.634
Step one writes sequel P
R D oh one's login plan.

82
00:04:41.634 --> 00:04:44.785
Four legacy logins map to
four Entra principals.

83
00:04:44.785 --> 00:04:48.735
The create script starts with a
dry-run comment: nothing here has run.

84
00:04:48.735 --> 00:04:52.390
Notice that S Q L dash D B A
dash Admins gets sys-admin,

85
00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:55.610
because the Windows
group it replaces had it.

86
00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.172
The S A account and the built-in
service login aren't in the plan.

87
00:04:59.172 --> 00:05:04.185
Then the summary: four logins mapped, none
unresolved, and the create script's hash.

88
00:05:04.185 --> 00:05:05.572
And three warnings.

89
00:05:05.572 --> 00:05:09.865
Payroll web M I and the payroll users
group would each hold three roles,

90
00:05:09.865 --> 00:05:12.160
and the D B A group would hold sys-admin.

91
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.773
The plan is still written.

92
00:05:13.773 --> 00:05:16.202
Splitting the groups,
or recording why not,

93
00:05:16.202 --> 00:05:20.120
is the identity engineer's call
before the mapping is approved.

94
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.595
Step two reads the
cutover and the rollback.

95
00:05:26.595 --> 00:05:30.733
The user and the sequel login are
disabled, and the rollback enables them.

96
00:05:30.733 --> 00:05:34.722
Contoso payroll users and Contoso
sequel admins are Windows groups,

97
00:05:34.722 --> 00:05:38.432
so their lines are deny connect
sequel and grant connect sequel.

98
00:05:38.432 --> 00:05:43.258
Alter login disable can't deny a Windows
group, and a server-permission deny can.

99
00:05:43.258 --> 00:05:46.342
Contoso sequel admins is
also a sys-admin member,

100
00:05:46.342 --> 00:05:49.271
and a deny doesn't apply
to members of sys-admin.

101
00:05:49.271 --> 00:05:53.608
So the cutover takes the group out of the
role first, and the rollback puts it back.

102
00:05:53.608 --> 00:05:55.250
Nothing is dropped.

103
00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:03.763
Step three leaves one login out of the
mapping: the sequel login payroll report.

104
00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:06.757
It loads the kit's module,
reads the day-one snapshot,

105
00:06:06.757 --> 00:06:10.163
drops that row from the mapping,
and builds the plan again.

106
00:06:10.163 --> 00:06:13.360
The create script now
carries an unresolved line:

107
00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.700
map it, or record why it isn't migrated.

108
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.162
And the cutover leaves payroll report out.

109
00:06:18.162 --> 00:06:22.762
Its disable line is gone, so the login
keeps working until someone maps it.

110
00:06:22.762 --> 00:06:25.580
The plan never guesses an Entra principal.

111
00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.105
Step four judges the
parallel run on day one.

112
00:06:32.105 --> 00:06:35.592
Nothing connects through Entra
yet, so nothing is ready:

113
00:06:35.592 --> 00:06:38.130
zero of four, keep the parallel run going.

114
00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:39.467
Read the Why column.

115
00:06:39.467 --> 00:06:42.468
Every Entra principal is
not yet seen connecting.

116
00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:47.606
Contoso S V C payroll still has
forty-one remote N T L M sessions.

117
00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:51.605
And three legacy logins still carry
traffic that arrives nowhere else.

118
00:06:51.605 --> 00:06:53.955
Now look at Contoso sequel admins.

119
00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:56.243
It shows zero legacy sessions.

120
00:06:56.243 --> 00:06:58.033
Its one session was local,

121
00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:02.360
and local sessions always report
N T L M, so they don't count.

122
00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.148
Step five judges day thirty.

123
00:07:08.148 --> 00:07:10.968
Every Entra principal is seen connecting,

124
00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:15.561
and no legacy session remains: four
of four, every exit criterion met.

125
00:07:15.561 --> 00:07:17.823
This snapshot is after the cutover.

126
00:07:17.823 --> 00:07:21.210
Contoso S V C payroll and
payroll report are disabled,

127
00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.059
and the two groups are
denied connect sequel.

128
00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:28.697
The judge reads that state, and says
what runbook R five says comes next:

129
00:07:28.697 --> 00:07:30.935
observe for thirty days, then drop.

130
00:07:30.935 --> 00:07:32.950
Nothing has been dropped yet.

131
00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.475
Step six opens the day-thirty manifest.

132
00:07:39.475 --> 00:07:44.443
It names the step, parallel, its
mode, read-only, its controls,

133
00:07:44.443 --> 00:07:49.462
A C two and C A seven, its K S I,
and the hash of each file it wrote.

134
00:07:49.462 --> 00:07:51.524
Step seven re-hashes the plan.

135
00:07:51.524 --> 00:07:55.738
Every file's recorded hash matches
the file on disk, and that matters:

136
00:07:55.738 --> 00:07:59.425
the hash a reviewer approves is
the hash of the files on disk.

137
00:07:59.425 --> 00:08:03.325
Step eight runs this chapter's
tests: eleven pass, none fail.

138
00:08:03.325 --> 00:08:05.212
And step nine lists your runs.

139
00:08:05.212 --> 00:08:06.750
There's nothing to clean up.

140
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.812
The kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

141
00:08:08.812 --> 00:08:11.610
and this run's results
stay in its own folder.

142
00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.448
Lab 2 runs one mapping on
your own test instance.

143
00:08:18.448 --> 00:08:20.185
It's not run by the authors.

144
00:08:20.185 --> 00:08:24.907
You need a test instance, never
production: Sequel Server 2022 or later,

145
00:08:24.907 --> 00:08:29.711
not a failover cluster instance,
Arc-enabled in a commercial Azure test

146
00:08:29.711 --> 00:08:32.723
subscription, which is
where a Microsoft 365

147
00:08:32.723 --> 00:08:35.410
G C C Moderate tenant's
Arc resources live.

148
00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:39.379
It needs Entra authentication
configured, an Entra admin set,

149
00:08:39.379 --> 00:08:42.647
as in Module 3, and a
certificate your client trusts.

150
00:08:42.647 --> 00:08:45.305
Write a one-row mapping
for one Windows login,

151
00:08:45.305 --> 00:08:48.322
read the create script, and
run it as the Entra admin.

152
00:08:48.322 --> 00:08:50.698
That's the only step
that changes anything.

153
00:08:50.698 --> 00:08:53.435
Then sign in as a member
of the test Entra group,

154
00:08:53.435 --> 00:08:56.461
validate a real Entra
connection, and judge the run.

155
00:08:56.461 --> 00:09:00.527
To back it out, sign in as the
Entra admin, drop the database users

156
00:09:00.527 --> 00:09:04.360
the script created, then the
login, and delete the lab's files.

157
00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.930
Drop login fails while
the login is connected,

158
00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.760
so wait for the member's session
to close and run that line again.

159
00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.160
The federal requirement.

160
00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:24.603
Chapter 4's table maps the login plan to
NIST SP 800-53 A C two, Account Management,

161
00:09:24.603 --> 00:09:27.463
C M three, Configuration Change Control,

162
00:09:27.463 --> 00:09:30.410
and C M four, Impact
Analyses, with two K S Is.

163
00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.908
The plan also supports I A two,
Identification and Authentication

164
00:09:34.908 --> 00:09:38.998
for organizational users, which
has no K S I pairing in the kit.

165
00:09:38.998 --> 00:09:43.168
The evidence is the reviewed create,
cutover and rollback scripts,

166
00:09:43.168 --> 00:09:47.111
the plan file, the mapping, and
the manifest with each file's hash.

167
00:09:47.111 --> 00:09:52.535
The parallel-run judge supports A C two
and C A seven, Continuous Monitoring.

168
00:09:52.535 --> 00:09:56.540
Its evidence is the parallel file
for each snapshot with its manifest,

169
00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:00.822
kept with the Sequel Server Audit files
runbook R five keeps for the window.

170
00:10:00.822 --> 00:10:05.050
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

171
00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:08.510
your providers report against
them, the agency doesn't.

172
00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.146
The records provide
evidence for a control;

173
00:10:11.146 --> 00:10:15.550
the agency sets each control's
parameters, and the assessor decides.

174
00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.400
Check yourself with two questions.

175
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:25.163
First: why does sequel P R D oh
one's cutover deny connect sequel

176
00:10:25.163 --> 00:10:29.275
to the Contoso payroll users group,
instead of disabling the login?

177
00:10:29.275 --> 00:10:30.274
The answer is a.

178
00:10:30.274 --> 00:10:33.437
Alter login disable can't
deny a Windows group:

179
00:10:33.437 --> 00:10:36.100
it returns error fifteen one fifty-one.

180
00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:38.113
A server-permission deny can.

181
00:10:38.113 --> 00:10:41.700
Second: a legacy login has
been disabled at cutover.

182
00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.050
When can it be dropped?

183
00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:47.050
The answer is b: after thirty days'
observation, when the audit record,

184
00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.550
with failed logins for the disabled
login, shows nothing still depends on it.

185
00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:56.200
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

186
00:10:60.000 --> 00:11:01.025
To sum up.

187
00:11:01.025 --> 00:11:04.770
A reviewed mapping becomes Entra
logins beside the Windows ones,

188
00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:07.863
as T-SQL that executes
nothing until it's approved.

189
00:11:07.863 --> 00:11:12.085
The parallel run decides from evidence
when a legacy login can be turned off,

190
00:11:12.085 --> 00:11:15.100
and nothing is dropped until
thirty days' observation.

191
00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.799
You wrote a fictional
instance's plan and judged

192
00:11:17.799 --> 00:11:20.363
its parallel run before
and after the cutover.

193
00:11:20.363 --> 00:11:24.967
The I S S O keeps the reviewed scripts,
the plan, the mapping and the manifest

194
00:11:24.967 --> 00:11:29.950
with each hash, and the parallel file for
each snapshot, kept with the audit files.

195
00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:32.925
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 4's checklist.

196
00:11:32.925 --> 00:11:37.410
Next, Module 5: groups,
roles and the perimeter.
